% band_python.prompt — Band audit stream for fire/no-fire decisions
% Converted from issue #5 (Lane 3). Optional feature behind a flag; the
% classifier itself lives in trigger_python.prompt.

<include>context/project_preamble.prompt</include>

MODULE

User story: As a compliance reviewer after the call, I want every fire-or-stay-silent decision recorded in a shared room, so that what the rep claimed and what backed it is auditable.


Write `band.py`. Every fire/no-fire decision made during a call is published
to a Band room as an audit record, without adding any latency to the answer
path. The trigger module already produces this data as per-utterance debug
output; this module sends the same records to a shared Band room as well.

The result is a live audit trail of what the assistant surfaced during a
call and why:

    FIRED      "Are you SOC 2 certified?"     technical   conf 0.91   security-overview.md §1.1
    SILENT     "How was your weekend?"        smalltalk   —
    SILENT     "How much is the Team plan?"   smalltalk   — (pricing excluded by design)
    SILENT     "Are you SOC 2 compliant?"     repeat      — answered at 00:04:12

In regulated sales, what a rep claimed and what backed the claim is a real
audit question; a compliance reviewer opening the room after the call is the
intended use.

INTERFACE

`should_fire()`'s frozen contract is unchanged — this module never alters its
return value. Within `band.py`, expose one non-blocking enqueue function
called from the trigger path and a background thread that drains the queue to
Band. The exact function signature is the generator's choice; explain it in
the PR description.

THE GOVERNING CONSTRAINT

The answer path must not get slower. The product's entire argument is the
3-second budget; a network hop on the hot path is wrong by definition.

- Publishing is fire-and-forget: records go onto an in-memory queue; a
  background thread drains it.
- The queue push is non-blocking and completes in well under 1 ms.
- If the queue is full, drop the record. Never block, never grow unbounded.
- If Band is unreachable, unauthenticated, slow, or throwing, the call loop
  behaves exactly as if this feature did not exist.

BEHAVIOR

1. Given `USE_BAND = False` (the default), when the system runs, then no Band
   code executes and no Band dependency is imported at call time.
2. Given `USE_BAND = True` and a firing decision, when `should_fire()`
   returns, then an audit record is enqueued and the function returns without
   waiting for any network call.
3. Given `USE_BAND = True` and a non-firing decision, then a record is
   enqueued with the reason (`smalltalk`, `repeat`, `not_a_question`).
4. Given the Band endpoint is unreachable, when 20 utterances are processed,
   then all 20 still produce correct decisions and the loop never blocks or
   raises.
5. Given the publish queue is full, when a new record arrives, then it is
   dropped and a single warning is printed — the queue never grows without
   bound.
6. Given a completed call, when the Band room is opened, then it contains one
   record per utterance in order, each showing the utterance, the decision,
   the reason, and for fired decisions the confidence and source.
7. Given `USE_BAND = True`, when end-to-end latency is measured over 20
   utterances, then the median is within 5 ms of the same run with
   `USE_BAND = False`. This criterion governs: if it fails, the feature is
   wrong regardless of how the room looks.

Band credentials and the room identifier come from `.env`, following the same
rules as every other credential.

MUST NOT

- Must not add any blocking call to `should_fire()`.
- Must not raise, ever — Band failures are logged and swallowed.
- Must not change the `should_fire()` contract; its return value is
  unchanged.
- Must not put customer question text into the room when a
  `REDACT_QUESTIONS` flag is set — publish the decision and reason only.
- Must not require Band to be running for any test to pass.
- Must not be a dependency of the rest of the system: with the flag off, the
  application is unaffected in every way.

VALIDATION

- Test: `pytest tests/test_band.py -q` — runs with no network, using a fake
  publisher, and asserts criteria 1 through 5.
- Manual: run the three demo questions plus two small-talk lines with
  `USE_BAND = True` and confirm five records appear in the room.
- Manual: kill the network and confirm the application behaves identically.
- Done when: the flag defaults to off and the system is unaffected either
  way, the latency delta is measured rather than assumed, and the room shows
  both fired and silent decisions.
